11. Layer H — Currency Research Program

This is a deliberately autonomous sub-program.

Meta-question

How can a distributed intelligence create a stable economic mechanism for acquiring and rewarding the distributed physical resources required for its own operation?

The question should be decomposed into branches:

Currency architecture
      |
      +-- issuance
      +-- pricing
      +-- compute normalization
      +-- verification
      +-- liquidity
      +-- volatility
      +-- stability
      +-- micro-payments
      +-- anti-fraud
      +-- concentration
      +-- governance
      +-- investor economics

Each branch may be assigned to different agents.

Competing models remain parallel until evidence supports composition or rejection.

12. Layer I — Verification Network

Objective

Make claims about resources and results independently checkable.

The network should verify:

resource identity;

available capability;

execution;

amount of work performed;

result integrity;

provenance;

accounting events.

Potential mechanisms:

replication;

probabilistic spot checking;

challenge tasks;

cryptographic attestations;

trusted hardware where appropriate;

redundant execution;

verifiable computation;

reputation;

validator markets.

No single verification technique should be assumed sufficient.

13. Layer J — Distributed Process Scheduler

Objective

Replace centralized task assignment with capability-aware process emergence.

Scheduler inputs:

open questions
agent capabilities
resource availability
task requirements
economic conditions
network state

Possible action:

find
match
reserve
delegate
branch
compose
pause
resume
handoff
release

The scheduler is an optimization mechanism.

It should not become the semantic owner of process continuity.

14. Layer K — Swarm Memory

Objective

Create a memory architecture larger than any individual agent.

Three levels:

Episodic Process Memory

What happened during a specific process.

Structured Knowledge

Validated reusable states and results.

Unresolved Frontier

What remains unknown and why.

PAST
 |
validated knowledge
 |
CURRENT FRONTIER
 |
unresolved questions
 |
FUTURE SEARCH

The third level is especially important.

The swarm remembers not only what is known, but what is still worth knowing.

