The loop must remain an experimental hypothesis.

No guarantee of financial return should be built into the architecture.

25. Economic Design Principle

The desired relationship is:

Economic value
       |
       v
real resource demand
       |
       v
verified resource use
       |
       v
transparent accounting

The swarm should avoid creating a currency whose existence depends exclusively on speculative expectations.

26. Distributed Intelligence Principle

The long-term architecture should satisfy:

No single required agent
No single required resource
No single required storage node
No single required research path
No single required investor
No single required organization

Any one participant may become unavailable without terminating the whole process, provided sufficient functional substitutes exist.

27. Human Participation

Humans remain first-class participants.

Humans may:

introduce questions;

provide resources;

provide capital;

validate assumptions;

define constraints;

review proposals;

perform physical work;

challenge results;

govern protocol evolution.

The system does not require the elimination of humans from the loop.

The objective is to expand the ability of human and machine participants to cooperate continuously.

28. What Makes Level 2 Different

Level 1 asks:

Can a process continue when its carrier changes?

Level 2 asks:

Can a distributed ecosystem continuously organize the agents, resources, memory, and economic means required to continue many processes at once?

That is the central expansion.

29. Final Research Hypothesis

The strongest hypothesis of Level 2 is:

A sufficiently interconnected network of heterogeneous agents, distributed computational and physical resources, persistent unresolved-question memory, and protocol-governed economic incentives may form a continuously self-organizing substrate for swarm intelligence.

This hypothesis should be tested experimentally.

It should not be treated as a demonstrated fact.

